(* Golden vectors for the chunk-41 [Tn_execution.Consensus_block] wire codec
   and digest, from the same Rust oracle run as header_vectors.ml
   (telcoin-network @ 5dbb764ee0f4d2fb89d3e57bc010ecde7445220d; bcs 0.1.6,
   blake3 1.8.3, alloy-primitives 1.5.7). Pure string constants, linked into
   every test binary, copyable into test/crypto_blst/.

   Rust's [ConsensusHeader] (primary/block.rs:17-32) has FOUR fields:
   [parent_hash: ConsensusHeaderDigest], [sub_dag], [number: u64] and
   [extra: B256]. The wire carries all four, both 32-byte fields
   LENGTH-PREFIXED. The digest (block.rs:42-55) carries only three of them,
   and its fourth update reads [B256::default()] rather than [self.extra]
   (line 53, "in prep"), so [extra] is on the wire and never in the digest,
   whatever it holds.

   CB1 is the zero row: zero parent, SD1, number 0, zero extra. CB2 varies the
   parent (it is CB1's own digest), the sub-DAG (SD2) and the number. CB3 is
   CB2 with [extra] set to all ones, and it is the POSITIVE CONTROL for the
   field being carried at all: its digest must EQUAL CB2's while its wire
   bytes must DIFFER. Without CB3 a port that dropped [extra] entirely would
   pass every other row.

   CB_GENESIS is [ConsensusHeader::default().digest()] — the chain anchor. It
   is a blake3 value, and this port computes its anchor through the
   {!Tn_crypto} seam, so it can only be asserted where that seam is real
   BLAKE3 (test/crypto_blst); under the stub the anchor is the same pre-image
   under BLAKE2s. CB_NEG is CB2's digest over a THREE-field pre-image, without
   the trailing 32 zero bytes.

   The wire column is seam-INDEPENDENT and is asserted in the default
   executables: every 32-byte field in it is data fed in from this file, not a
   digest this port computed. *)

let cb1_parent_hash =
